# K1 / B010 — Run 2 replica annotation (blind).

C R251 OL DEP-009,DEP-001 | MUST | Use the persistence provider the project actually selected; never add a provider because an example shows it. | EF Core registration | none stated
C R252 OL ARC-006,ARC-003 | MUST NOT | Infrastructure pipeline hooks — EF Core interceptors and HTTP DelegatingHandlers — are used only for infrastructure-level cross-cutting behavior with a clear benefit (auditing, auth headers, correlation, request signing); never hide business behavior or business decisions in them. | Backend infrastructure code | none stated
C R253 OL GOV-006 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | SQL Server string comparison follows column/database collation (default case-insensitive); choosing case sensitivity or a collation is a product decision when observable, and an approved choice is made explicit with UseCollation(...) or a column-level collation. | SQL Server persistence | none stated
C X38 EX RUN-002,ARC-022 | MUST NOT | Never fire-and-forget EF Core asynchronous operations; a DbContext is not thread-safe. | EF Core data access | none stated | RUN-002 forbids blocking async I/O and ARC-022 governs DI lifetimes; no oracle policy forbids un-awaited (fire-and-forget) database operations or concurrent DbContext use. | Starting an EF Core query without awaiting it (e.g. background Task.Run over the request DbContext) is prohibited.
C X39 EX RUN-016,DEP-020 | MUST NOT | Do not wrap normal database operations in a generic application-level retry pipeline or apply an HTTP retry policy to them; database transient faults use the persistence provider's execution strategy (EnableRetryOnFailure), with explicit transactions wrapped in the execution strategy. | Database operations | none stated | RUN-016 limits retries to safe operations and DEP-020 sets the default resilience packages, but no oracle policy prescribes provider-level execution strategies or forbids application-level retry pipelines for database calls (the oracle treats database-retry examples as technique, EXC-007). | A Polly/Microsoft resilience pipeline around repository calls is prohibited; SQL Server retry goes through EnableRetryOnFailure.
C R254 OL GOV-019 | MUST NOT | Never discard, overwrite, reset, clean, stash away or otherwise destroy user work to make a Git operation easier. | Git operations | none stated
C R255 OL GOV-020 | MUST | Before any repository-changing operation inspect the relevant state (git status, git diff, git diff --cached) and use the minimum operation required; never silently clean unrelated working-tree changes. | Git operations | none stated
C R256 OL GOV-015,GOV-020 | MUST | Treat git reset --hard, git clean, git push --force/--force-with-lease, branch deletion and rebasing shared history as high-risk: perform them only when explicitly requested (or clearly required by an approved workflow) and only after inspecting what will be lost or rewritten. | Git operations | 'clearly required by an approved workflow' (see UNCERTAIN U3)
C U3 LU GOV-015 | MAY (exception) | High-risk Git operations may be performed when 'clearly required by an approved workflow', not only when explicitly requested. | High-risk Git operations | none stated | Open question: GOV-015 permits repository-changing Git operations only when explicitly requested; whether 'clearly required by an approved workflow' merely refers to a workflow the user explicitly requested (entailed) or lets a workflow trigger destructive operations without a specific request (a relaxation) is not determinable from the text. | Under the broad reading, a workflow could reset or force-push without the user asking for that specific operation.
C R257 OL WF-022 | MUST | Before committing inspect the staged diff: it contains only the intended logical change; never stage or clean unrelated files merely because they are modified; prefer one logical change per commit with implementation and its tests together, no artificial tiny commits and no combined unrelated fixes/refactors. | Git commits | none stated
C R258 OL GOV-016,GOV-019,GOV-014 | MUST | Follow the repository's branch-naming convention (defaults feature/, fix/, refactor/, test/, docs/, chore/), do not rename an existing user branch to satisfy a convention, never check one branch out in multiple normal worktrees, keep each worktree focused, and never remove a worktree containing uncommitted user work (verify preservation first). | Git branches and worktrees | none stated
C R259 OL GOV-015,GOV-020,WF-003,WF-022 | MUST | Before pushing confirm the intended branch and understood working/staged state, ensure relevant verification completed and avoid pushing unrelated local commits; never force-push a shared branch unless the user explicitly requests history rewriting and understands the consequences. | git push | Explicit request for history rewriting.
C X40 EX GOV-015 | MUST NOT | Treat main, master and other protected/shared branches as non-rewritable. | Git history rewriting | none stated | GOV-015 allows force-push and other history-changing operations when explicitly requested; declaring main/master non-rewritable is stricter (read literally, even an explicit request does not unlock rewriting them). | Force-pushing main is refused or treated as forbidden even when requested.
C R260 OL GOV-019,GOV-020,WF-003 | MUST | For merge/rebase follow the repository/team convention or the user's explicit request, inspect local uncommitted work first and never automatically stash, reset or discard it; resolve conflicts from the intended combined behavior rather than mechanically choosing ours/theirs, and run relevant verification afterwards. | git merge/rebase | none stated
C R261 OL WF-003,WF-009,GOV-014 | MUST | Run verify before preparing a PR; a PR has a focused purpose without accidental unrelated changes; never claim checks that were not run. | Pull requests | none stated
C X41 EX WF-009 | MUST | A pull request description explains what changed, why, how it was verified and compatibility/migration notes. | Pull requests | none stated | WF-009 covers truthful verification reporting; no oracle policy fixes PR description content. | Preparing a PR without a why/verification/compatibility section is non-conformant.
C R262 OL DEP-011,DEP-020 | DEFAULT | Resilience mechanisms are added only when failure and operation semantics justify them (standard handler not mandatory for every client; no stacked handlers with overlapping strategies); configure them through the Microsoft.Extensions resilience APIs. | Outbound HTTP resilience | none stated
C X42 EX RUN-005 | MUST NOT | Apply per-request values such as dynamic access tokens to the specific request or through a handler; never mutate a shared HttpClient's DefaultRequestHeaders concurrently. | Outbound HttpClient usage | Stable headers (media types) may be configured on the client. | RUN-005 governs HttpClient lifetime only; no oracle policy forbids mutating shared default headers. | Setting Authorization on DefaultRequestHeaders of a shared typed client per request is prohibited.

